{
  "sourceNullable": "{\"cells\":[{\"cell_type\":\"markdown\",\"id\":\"c00\",\"metadata\":{},\"source\":\"# Google's three quantum jobs, 22.87 times cheaper, every answer checked on a CPU\\n\\n*Google's three quantum jobs, done on a bill 22.87 times smaller on average, every answer re-checked here by\\nGoogle's own verifiers on a Kaggle CPU. This zip is Ahmed Berat Ozer's public V14 output of 16:06 UTC on\\n2026-10-29, the cheapest public file per job our field watcher had verified at 16:23 UTC, with one file\\nchanged by us: the chemistry weights, refit at fixed words. Two pieces carry our work: that weights file and the fusion file\\n(mrmeows' public circuit with two regions rebuilt by our searches), which his output already carried byte for byte.\\nhengck23's 889-qubit message-repair decoder (chosen in public by kirilltym), Atakan Aldemir's chemistry\\ncircuits and Ahmed Berat Ozer's V14 weights, which our refit starts from, are credited as his notebook names them.\\nkirilltym's team LB, 22.85529, carries the same decoder bytes and reads the hosts' salt for them: one failed\\nshot. So these bytes read 22.86842 by arithmetic.*\\n\\nGoogle Quantum AI wrote three circuits for the error-corrected (\\\"fault-tolerant\\\") quantum computers its paper is\\nabout: one for fusion physics, one that repairs a damaged coded message, one for chemistry. Each circuit comes with a\\nbill: how many qubits it holds at its busiest moment, and how many expensive gates it runs. This notebook hands in\\ncircuits that do the same three jobs on a bill 22.87 times smaller on average, and has Google's own checking\\nprograms re-test every answer here, on an ordinary Kaggle CPU. The receipt below is printed by this run.\\n\\n| | |\\n|---|---|\\n| result | **22.86842**, by the hosts' metric code, our local run of these bytes (Public split, our salts) (22.86841 truncated as Kaggle shows scores: the title's number, not an LB read); LB of these bytes: not read yet |\\n| what it means | the geometric mean, over the three jobs, of Google's bill divided by ours (the hosts' metric) |\\n| the salt's share | the decoder in this zip met k = 1 failed shots on our Public salt, k = 1 on our Private salt (22.86843 there), k = 1 unsalted (our verifier and hengck23's own receipt) and k = 0 on kirilltym's salt; each failure adds about half a percent to the decoder's bill (chapter 5). The verifier seeds its shots from the SHA-256 of the circuit file merged with the salt, so each file draws its own k on the hosts' secret salt, and only an LB of the same file reads it. kirilltym's 22.85529 reads this file at k = 1 (our fusion file, this decoder and the chemistry before our refit, and no other k: k = 0 would read 22.89023), while two LBs read the SomeJoint team's 889-qubit file at k = 0 (Ahmed Berat Ozer's team's 22.80996 and the SomeJoint team's 22.80621). By arithmetic these bytes read **22.86842** on the LB (chapter 7) |\\n| ours: chemistry weights | Ahmed Berat Ozer's V14 weights file (his update of Atakan Aldemir's factor), as his output carries it, with two of its arrays refit by us at fixed words: a constrained refit, then a column gauge, the method of our weights of 2026-10-27 and of our sub_023. His errors sit at 19.9994 and 19.9992 mHa (limit 20; 5.90e-07 and 7.54e-07 Ha of slack); ours at 19.9997 and 19.9998 mHa, and each of the three accuracy checks passes with at least 2.1e-07 Ha to spare. Volume 48.476421 to 48.476051, a chemistry bill -0.00076% against his, x1.0000025 on the score, +0.000058 on these bytes; the official x86-64 verifier gives the cost of our Mac runs to one unit in the last place (relative 1.76e-16; chapter 6) |\\n| ours: fusion | mrmeows' 63-seat fusion file with two regions rebuilt by our searches: the main lookup (172 AND gates in place of 186, our V7 and V8), then window S1 (25 in place of 30, since our V9). Both steps move the fusion bill by -1.156%; window S1 alone is x1.0010248 on the score, +0.023411 on these bytes. It went public at 00:01 UTC, and 23 of the 26 public outputs we harvested from runs of 2026-10-29 up to 16:23 UTC carry it byte for byte (chapter 4) |\\n| hengck23's: message repair | his 889-seat decoder (his `dqi_iter014` build, on the SomeJoint team's line), unchanged: 433,784.89 stamps per shot (our verifier, unsalted, equal to his receipt) against 437,947.61 for the SomeJoint team's 889-seat file; the same seats with 4,163 fewer stamps, a decoder bill -0.951% against theirs at equal k. One failed shot adds 0.46%, so at the k each file meets on the hosts' salt (1 here, 0 for theirs) it is still -0.496%, x1.0016575 on the score. Our field watcher took it from kirilltym's public output (run 14:08 UTC); Ahmed Berat Ozer's output carries the same bytes (chapter 5) |\\n| Atakan Aldemir's: chemistry circuits | his 15 natively emitted circuits (two AND reductions he credits to heliosli, one to Dmitrii Gluzdov), unchanged: 16,270 stamps against 16,274 in Dmitrii Gluzdov's circuits of 12:58 UTC and 16,276 in those of our sub_023. With his own weights the chemistry bill is -0.0516% against Dmitrii Gluzdov's and -0.0960% against our sub_023's; Ahmed Berat Ozer's V14 weights move it a further -0.1714% (chapter 6) |\\n| per job (our salts) | fusion 20.29x (cost 102,349.22) \u00b7 message repair 59.57x (4.10275e11) \u00b7 chemistry 9.89x (339,143,297.04) |\\n| ours, measured, not in this zip | on 2026-10-28 our loader relations (heliosli's method, our search) moved the SomeJoint team's chemistry bill by -0.4719% (their earlier words) and -0.0489% (their newer); heliosli's plans for the newer words moved it by -0.3666%, and the public circuits since have gone further (Atakan Aldemir's are here). Our chemistry weights of 2026-10-27 went byte for byte into the 22.00949 zip of that day (chapters 6 and 8) |\\n| the race | LB 22.00949 (2026-10-27): SomeJoint's and manwithacat's zip with our weights. LB 22.33981: our V8. LB 22.72431: our V9 and V10. On 2026-10-29, LB 22.79257 for our sub_022 and 22.80413 for our sub_023, both with Mudit Chaturvedi's decoder. On the 2026-10-29 15:19:32 UTC board kirilltym's team reads 22.85529 (rank 136), Ahmed Berat Ozer's 22.80996, the SomeJoint team's 22.80621, heliosli's 22.80578, Dmitrii Gluzdov's 22.80258 and alex chilton's 22.79549, and the latest output of each of their notebooks that we harvested up to 16:23 UTC carries our fusion file. This zip: LB not read yet; 22.86842 by arithmetic (this decoder at k = 1 on the hosts' salt, as kirilltym's LB reads it), which would stand at rank 128 of 1,555 on the 2026-10-29 15:19:32 UTC board; our row there reads 22.80413 at rank 228, from sub_023 (chapter 8) |\\n| how we got here | our first LB 9.73274; then 17.53289 on our own circuits (message repair 31.69x, chemistry 8.87x); then 21.88210 with our chemistry weights on the strongest public circuits; 452 recorded attempts (ledgers as of 2026-10-29 17:52 UTC) in 94 hours (chapter 7) |\\n| runtime | 16 min on 4 Kaggle CPUs, no GPU, internet off (private dry run, version 18) |\\n| built by | fusion: mrmeows (team selenium), with two regions rebuilt by us (**busyaprime**) \u00b7 message repair: hengck23, his 889-qubit decoder, on the SomeJoint team's line, chosen in public by kirilltym \u00b7 chemistry: Atakan Aldemir's circuits, Ahmed Berat Ozer's V14 weights, refit by us (**busyaprime**) \u00b7 zip layout: our field watcher's writer (fixed stamps); full credits at the end |\\n\\n<sub>sources: hosts' metric record `data/qcc/runs/sub_028/metric_local/metric_local.json`; our submissions list `data/qcc/lb/2026-10-29T1519/33_our_submissions_2026-10-29T1519.csv` (sub_022's and sub_023's LBs); board `data/qcc/lb/2026-10-29T1519/x/google-verifiable-quantum-circuit-compilation-publicleaderboard-2026-10-29T15:19:32.csv` (our row, kirilltym's, Ahmed Berat Ozer's, the SomeJoint team's, heliosli's, Dmitrii Gluzdov's and alex chilton's LBs); the watcher's best-of `data/qcc/watch/best_snapshot_2026-10-29T1623.json` (pinned copy of its `best.json`: the pick, its sources, run times and prices) and its prices pinned at 15:19 UTC `data/qcc/watch/prices_snapshot_2026-10-29T1519.json` (the LB fits on the 15:19:32 board); Ahmed Berat Ozer's zip `e883b87d...` and log in `data/qcc/public/w_ahmedberatozer__quantum-ai-df-thc-group-compression__2026-10-29T160632/out/` and his notebook text `data/qcc/v8/sub028/ah_pull/` (the authors it names, his weights update); kirilltym's zip `f23332e9...` and log in `data/qcc/public/w_kirilltym__quantum-circuits-exact-gauge-5-word-qrom__2026-10-29T140824/out/` and his notebook text `data/qcc/v8/sub027/kt_pull/` (the authors it names, the 18 hashes it pins, its decoder receipts); hengck23's build record and receipt `data/qcc/v8/sub027/hengck_ds/files/`, his notebook text `data/qcc/v8/sub027/hengck_pull/`; Atakan Aldemir's notebook text `data/qcc/v8/sub027/atakan_pull/` and his source bank's metadata `data/qcc/v8/sub027/atakan_ds/`; our weights file: `data/qcc/circuits/dfthcsos_r39_A.provenance.json`, its official x86-64 runs `data/qcc/work/dfthcsos/r39a/official/` and the critic's `data/qcc/work/critic/R39/official/`, critic report `competitions/33_qcc/critic/33_CRITIC_R39.md`; the SomeJoint team's 889-qubit file: the watcher's price in the same pinned prices; our sub_023: `data/qcc/runs/sub_023/metric_local/metric_local.json` and `data/qcc/work/dfthcsos/r34a/official_C/`; our dataset's version time `data/qcc/v8/sub022/our_datasets_2026-10-29T0607.csv`; fusion `data/qcc/circuits/invsqrt_r27_XAG2C.provenance.json`, `.verify.json`, `data/qcc/work/critic/R27-INV/official/`; our sub_022 and V9 and V10 history: `data/qcc/runs/sub_022/metric_local/metric_local.json`, `data/qcc/runs/sub_020c/metric_local/metric_local.json`, board `data/qcc/lb/2026-10-29T0035/x/`; attempt ledgers `competitions/33_qcc/*/33_attempt_ledger.jsonl`</sub>\"},{\"cell_type\":\"code\",\"id\":\"c01\",\"metadata\":{\"_kg_hide-input\":true,\"jupyter\":{\"source_hidden\":true,\"outputs_hidden\":true},\"_kg_hide-output\":